if prepend {
    correctedValue, key := node.popValue(), node.popKey()
    if node.IsLeaf {
        sibling.prependValue(correctedValue)
        sibling.prependKey(key)
        parent.replaceValueAt(i, node.lastValue())
    } else {
        parentValue := parent.valueAt(i)
        sibling.prependKey(key)
        sibling.prependValue(parentValue)
        parent.replaceValueAt(i, correctedValue)
    }
}
```

In the prepend branch, the `parent.replaceValueAt(i, ...)` call happens inside the if/else blocks, so the outer `value` variable wouldn't be used in that path. But in the else branch, after doing operations, it calls `parent.replaceValueAt(i, value)` using the `value` variable that was assigned earlier.

But there's still an issue: the `value` and `key` variables are declared outside the loop, and in subsequent iterations of the loop when `prepend` is true, these variables retain their old values from previous iterations where `prepend` was false.

This could lead to incorrect behavior when there are multiple iterations of the loop with alternating prepend/non-prepend operations.

